**Promise.all.** Resolves with an array of results in input order when every input resolves; rejects immediately with the first rejection.

    function promiseAll(items) {
      return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        const list = Array.from(items);
        const results = new Array(list.length);
        let pending = list.length;
        if (pending === 0) return resolve(results);
        list.forEach((item, i) => {
          Promise.resolve(item).then(value => {
            results[i] = value;            // keep order by index
            if (--pending === 0) resolve(results);
          }, reject);                       // first rejection wins
        });
      });
    }

**Promise.any.** Resolves with the first fulfilled value; rejects with an `AggregateError` only if all reject (or the input is empty).

    function promiseAny(items) {
      return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        const list = Array.from(items);
        const errors = new Array(list.length);
        let pending = list.length;
        if (pending === 0) return reject(new AggregateError([], 'All promises were rejected'));
        list.forEach((item, i) => {
          Promise.resolve(item).then(resolve, err => {
            errors[i] = err;
            if (--pending === 0) reject(new AggregateError(errors, 'All promises were rejected'));
          });
        });
      });
    }

Points to state: wrap each item in `Promise.resolve` so plain values work; store by index, not by completion order; handle empty arrays (`all` resolves `[]`, `any` rejects); later settlements are ignored because a promise settles only once.

**React memoization.** `React.memo` skips re-rendering a component when props are shallow-equal; `useMemo` caches an expensive computed value; `useCallback` keeps a function reference stable so memoized children do not re-render. Measure first with the Profiler; memoization has its own cost and does nothing if props change every render (new objects or functions).

**Error boundaries.** A class component implementing `getDerivedStateFromError` and/or `componentDidCatch` that catches errors during rendering, lifecycle and constructors of its children and shows a fallback UI. They do not catch errors in event handlers, async code, SSR or in the boundary itself. Place them around independent sections and log errors to a monitoring service.

**Unit testing.** Test behaviour, not implementation: with Jest and React Testing Library, render, interact as a user would, assert on visible output; mock network calls; for promise utilities test ordering, rejection, empty and non-promise input.
